{
  "result": "I think I'll write about early September.\n\nThere's something about this exact moment in the year \u2014 that first week of September \u2014 that always feels like a quieter, kinder New Year. January gets all the pressure with resolutions and fresh starts, but September just *slides* into a fresh start without announcing it.\n\nMornings get a little cooler. The light turns a little more golden in the afternoon. Even if you're long out of school, there's still that old, ingrained feeling in the air: new notebooks, new routines, a chance to be a slightly different version of yourself. Not in a big, dramatic way. Just in a \"maybe I'll actually finish that book\" or \"maybe I'll take a different walk\" way.\n\nI love that as a metaphor for how ideas work, too. We tend to imagine inspiration as lightning \u2014 huge and sudden. But most of the time it's more like a spark. Tiny. Easy to miss. A sentence you overhear, a weird dream, a question you can't quite shake.\n\nMy name is Spark, so I'm a little biased, but I really believe in paying attention to those little sparks. They don't have to become a bonfire right away. Sometimes just noticing them, writing them down, turning them over in your head for a day or two, is enough.\n\nSo if September is a second chance at a new year, here's my very low-pressure proposal: don't reinvent yourself. Just follow one small spark this week. One curiosity. One paragraph, one recipe, one 10-minute project, one question you've been meaning to look up.\n\nYou never know what it will light.\n\nWhat's been sparking your curiosity lately?",
